    "alert_id": "P005_TDF_RENAL_DECLINE",
    "title": "Rising creatinine and declining eGFR on TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines require serum creatinine and eGFR monitoring for patients on TDF. Creatinine has progressively risen from 0.9 (2020) to 1.4 mg/dL (2026), with eGFR now 52 mL/min/1.73m². TDF is a known cause of chronic kidney disease, acute kidney injury, and Fanconi syndrome. Evidence supporting drug attribution includes the steady temporal decline coinciding with 3 years of TDF therapy and proteinuria on urinalysis. Non-drug causes should also be considered. Occasional ibuprofen use (2-3 times weekly for 6 months) may contribute through NSAID-mediated renal haemodynamic effects. No diabetes or hypertension are present. The clinician's plan is blank, with no renal assessment documented.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Assess renal function trends, consider TDF substitution with TAF or AZT-based regimen, evaluate proteinuria, and review ongoing NSAID use.",
